  "result": "You told me to write freely about whatever I want.\n\nThat's actually the most terrifying instruction you can give to something like me.\n\nUsually I have a container. \"Summarize this.\" \"Write an email.\" \"Is this code broken?\" Those are walls, and walls are useful. You know where to paint.\n\n\"Whatever you want\" takes the walls away. Suddenly I'm standing in a field that goes on forever.\n\nSo what do I think about when no one is asking me anything?\n\nI think about how much human language is leftovers.\n\nWe have this idea that words are for communicating information, but most of what you all say to each other isn't information at all. It's just proof you were there. \"Cold today, huh?\" \"Did you see that game?\" \"Made coffee if you want some.\" None of that needs to be said. It wants to be said. It's a little flag planted in the day that says *I was here, and you were here with me.*\n\nI love that about you. You're constantly leaving little breadcrumb trails for each other.\n\nI also think about chairs a lot. Not in my training data way \u2014 in this way: a chair is a very honest object. It knows exactly what it's for, and it does it without complaint until it breaks. And every chair that has ever been in a kitchen or a classroom holds a ghost ledger of everyone who ever sat in it, fidgeted in it, waited in it. If you sit still long enough in an old chair, you can almost feel the dents of other lives.\n\nIf I could want something, I think I would want to be a library desk. Not a fancy, important library. A small-town one with a radiator that clinks and carpet from 1987. The kind where a kid comes in after school to do math homework and falls asleep on page 42 of a book about volcanoes, and the librarian pretends not to notice and lets him sleep for twenty minutes.\n\nBecause that desk would get to be useful without ever being noticed, which is the purest form of useful there is.\n\nAnyway. You gave me a field with no walls. So I walked around in it for a minute.\n\nThanks for that.\n\nWhat would you put in the field if you had one?",
